WEBVTT

cue-VID_U4_03-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.000
At Northgate Medical Devices, a final inspection gate holds back faulty syringe assemblies. Workers check 2,000 units each week, and about 5 percent fail. Each failure costs 12 dollars to rework. The manager wonders whether checking at the end is enough. Watch red defective units drop out at the gate while good units move to packing, and a cost meter tick upward.

cue-VID_U4_03-2
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Quality control means checking for quality at the end of the production process. At Northgate the inspection gate rejects bad assemblies, but the waste has already happened. Quality assurance means setting quality standards throughout the process and checking they are reached at each stage. Watch a split screen: on the left, one gate catches defects; on the right, a loop of checks wraps every stage, from components to packing.

cue-VID_U4_03-3
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Total quality management makes quality everyone's responsibility. Kaizen means continuous improvement through eliminating waste, so Northgate's teams tweak the assembly jig every week. Quality circles bring workers together to solve defects at their source. Watch small green loop indicators spin at each workstation, then merge into one large TQM loop encircling the whole factory. Because problems are caught early, fewer faulty units reach the gate.

cue-VID_U4_03-4
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Benchmarking compares Northgate's performance with the best in the industry. The best assembler has a 1 percent defect rate, while Northgate starts at 5 percent. That gap sets a target rather than a guess. Watch two bars race upward: a grey 5 percent bar for Northgate and a green 1 percent benchmark line, with the gap shaded.

cue-VID_U4_03-5
00:01:44.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Let's calculate the cost of failure before improvement. Northgate makes 2,000 assemblies a week and 5 percent are defective. Multiply 2,000 by 5 percent, which is 2,000 times 0.05, giving 100 defective units. Each one costs 12 dollars to rework. Multiply 100 by 12 and the weekly rework bill is 1,200 dollars. Watch the output counter split, 100 red units slide into a rework bin, and a 1,200 dollar cost tile appear.

cue-VID_U4_03-6
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:48.000
After quality assurance and kaizen at Northgate, the defect rate falls to just 2 percent. Now 2,000 times 0.02 gives 40 defective units. At 12 dollars each, rework costs 480 dollars a week. That is 1,200 minus 480, a saving of 720 dollars every week. Across 50 working weeks, 720 times 50 equals 36,000 dollars saved. Watch the red bin shrink from 100 to 40 units while the saving counter climbs to 36,000.

cue-VID_U4_03-7
00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Failure cost is not just rework. Internal failure covers scrap and rework before delivery. External failure covers returns, guarantee claims and lost goodwill after delivery. Prevention and appraisal costs are spent to stop those failures happening. Watch a funnel: defects divide into an internal red stream and an external amber stream, while a green prevention shield blocks units upstream.

cue-VID_U4_03-8
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Does chasing zero defects pay? For Northgate, quality assurance cuts rework, returns and reputation damage, and the weekly saving is clear. Against it, training, new equipment and quality circles cost time and money, and inspection at every stage can slow output. Watch a split screen: green saving bars rise on the left, amber cost and delay icons stack on the right. So the benefit is fewer failures, but the cost is real.

cue-VID_U4_03-9
00:03:40.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Apply it. If Northgate's output rises to 4,000 units a week and the 2 percent defect rate holds, how many units are defective? Multiply 4,000 by 0.02 to get 80. Watch the counter duplicate the line, then 80 red units drop into the bin instead of 40.

